<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dunwich</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dunwich is a village on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> that in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> was a major port and one of the largest towns in East Anglia. Storms from the late 13th century onwards and steady coastal erosion swept most of the town, including its churches, into the sea. It is traditionally identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dommoc">Dommoc</a>, the first East Anglian bishopric.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dunwich.jpg" alt="Dunwich" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Midnightblueowl · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dunwich,_Suffolk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dunwich lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> coast within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a> &amp; Essex Coast &amp; Heaths National Landscape, about 148 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, 14 kilometres south of Southwold and 11 kilometres north of Leiston. The port grew as a sheltered harbour where the River Dunwich met the sea. Storms and other coastal processes moved the river's mouth 4 kilometres north to Walberswick, and the town lost its reason for being; its sea defences were no longer kept up, and erosion steadily wore it away.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dommoc">Dommoc</a> was the seat founded by Sigeberht of East Anglia for Saint Felix in about 629–631 and held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> until about 870, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> took the kingdom; the historian Rory Naismith speaks of a bishopric of Dunwich, while Richard Hoggett places Dommoc at Walton Castle. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a> of 1086 gives Dunwich three churches and an estimated 3,000 people. The Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a> besieged it without success in 1173.</p><p>A storm surge on 1 January 1286 destroyed buildings at the town's eastern edge, and two more surges followed in February and December 1287. A storm in 1347 swept some 400 houses into the sea, and the Grote Mandrenke of about 16 January 1362 destroyed much of what was left. All eight medieval churches were lost in time: St Peter's fell between 1688 and 1697, and All Saints', abandoned in the 1750s, went over the cliff between 1904 and 1919. The borough kept the right to send two members to Parliament until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reform-act-1832">Reform Act 1832</a> and was one of the most notorious rotten boroughs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dunwich is now a small village with a parish meeting instead of a council. The ruins of the 13th-century Franciscan friary of Greyfriars and the Leper Hospital of St James survive, and a buttress of All Saints' tower stands in St James's Church, built in 1832. Sonar and diver surveys from 2008 have mapped the ruins of St Peter's and St Nicholas's churches under the sea, the largest medieval underwater site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and their results are shown in Dunwich Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dunwich” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later kings founded the bishopric of Dommoc, traditionally <b>Dunwich</b>, under Felix, and Anna's daughter Aethelthryth founded the monastery at Ely.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-east-anglia">Kingdom of East Anglia</a></li></ul></section>
